A||N||6||Will||Northstar clutch||||1||
Z||000000||Will||07-22-2001||03:23 PM||||I'm using a modified V6 flywheel on my Northstar. What clutch should I use with it?<BR>I'm leaning toward a Centerforce DF which I have been assured will hold 300 ftlbs. Experiences with this clutch? Alternatives?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Archie||07-22-2001||07:46 PM||||Ahhhhh.... I have a suggestion.||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||07-22-2001||08:37 PM||||Hi Will, the guys at chrfab calim to have a clutch and flywheel setup for our application. (!!)  There's the Centreforce, which will work, but I'm not a big fan of them (the flyweights and metal-metal friction material on flywheel side just make me nervours.  That said, we have one on a hotrodded 351 Windsor '67 Mustang and it's been great so far. 10.5" (I belive) disc holding over 400hp.<P>I am considering a twin-disc Quartermaster setup, ala California Kid's car ;) He has ~8kmiles on a very very powerful "liftinger" LT1.  At least, I am considering until I see pricing, heh.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000003||California Kid||07-22-2001||09:28 PM||||Hey Will, you have one very tough decision to make!!! I don't think anyone on this forum is going to say "Use this Clutch, it will hold up!!!", not after the string of posts on that other clutch matter.<P>I was going to post a note to see what other V8 guys were running (at my horsepower level), just because it would be nice to put a clutch in that would last 50,000 miles.<P>If you're running the Getrag Trans, then you're limited by the bell housing on what size disc you can put in (think it's between 9 to 10 inch for single disk).<P>I'm not saying "Use QuarterMasters Clutch Setup", only that their setup uses two 7.5 Clutch Discs, with Steel Floater in between them. That way, you effectively get approximately double the surface area of friction material in the tight confine of the bell housing.<P>I'm happy with my setup because I only use the car for cruise nights, shows, and pleasure driving during the summer (approx. 4-5,000 miles a year). I'm content to get two seasons out of this beast before it goes up on the hoist again. I have a "trusted shop" that installs the clutch for me, because it's a real pain to get the disc's lined up properly when installing trans. You have to eyeball the disc's, lightly torque the pressure plate bolts, install the trans, then very carefully remove trans, and finish the torque sequence on the pressure plate. If you do it correctly, the trans will go right into position.<P>Horsepower!!! You have to pay a price for everything !!! ||||reg||6
Z||000004||Will||07-22-2001||10:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>Ahhhhh.... I have a suggestion.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>...You mean other than putting in an SBC with an Archie kit and using the Archie/ Centerforce clutch?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I looked on the Centerforce website and noticed that they had your clutch listed. It said that the flywheel weight is 30 lbs. I know that the factory V8 flywheel is 30 lbs. I had thought that your flywheel was a custom piece. Is it a normal V8 flywheel, or a custom one with a different ring gear, or what? If it's a custom piece, why is it so heavy?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Archie||07-23-2001||08:11 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> ...You mean other than putting in an SBC with an Archie kit and using the Archie/ Centerforce clutch?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I looked on the Centerforce website and noticed that they had your clutch listed. It said that the flywheel weight is 30 lbs. I know that the factory V8 flywheel is 30 lbs. I had thought that your flywheel was a custom piece. Is it a normal V8 flywheel, or a custom one with a different ring gear, or what? If it's a custom piece, why is it so heavy?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I haven't gone around that web site checking out F/W weights for other cars, so any similarity in weight is coincidence. The profile (cross-section) of my F/W is unique and my own design. When I designed my kit, there was no stock GM F/W that could be modified to use in a Fiero V-8 swap, so I had to design my own.<P>Weight..... my F/W wasn't designed with any particular weight goal in mind. The F/W had to have a certain thickness, ring gear spacing & Diameter. When the thickness and diameter are controlled by the needed design elements & you want a steel F/W that will pass NHRA requirements. Weight is the last element you have any control over. I don't know the exact "shipping" weight of the F/W but I think that the F/W weighs like 28 pounds.<P>It doesn't matter anyway.....as this F/W will not work on a N-Star anyway.<P>Hope this helps.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||07-23-2001||08:58 PM||||What are NHRA requirements for a flywheel, or where can I find them?<BR>I know it won't work on a Northstar, I'm just satisfying my curiosity.<P>GM's standard V8 flywheel is ~30lbs.<BR>GM's lightweight flywheel is ~16 lbs (?).<BR>The V6 flywheel is about 12 lbs.<BR>I think the Fieroshop's (expen$ive) aluminum flywheel is about 8 lbs.||||reg||1
